n-lossless dtypes like Int64 to Int32 fall back to strict, and that this applies to both the lazy and streaming paths since type coercion happens during IR conversion.\n\nI'm checking the Int64-to-Int8 path: the lossless supertype comes back as Int64, not Int8, so the error branch fires and the cast stays strict. Next I want to see how the struct cast behaves in NonStrict mode when field names don't match, so I'm pulling up the struct cast_with_options code.\n\n", "thinking_blocks": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "I'm looking at how a strict cast in a lazy query with inputs gets downgraded to NonStrict when the policy says it's a safe cast. With missing struct fields inserted and extra fields ignored, a struct cast from {a,b} to {x,y} ends up as nulls, and {a,b} to {a} just drops the extra b.\n\nI'm checking the Int64-to-Int8 case: the integer upcast check passes, but the lossless supertype isn't Int8, so it errors and the cast stays strict. I'm also wondering whether this downgrade rule exists upstream in polars' type coercion, possibly as a lossless-cast optimization.\n\nI'm weighing whether the downgrade should use Raise for both the missing and extra struct field policies instead of Insert and Ignore. With Raise, the rename case {a,b}\u2192{x,y} would error and stay strict, and so would dropping b from {a,b}\u2192{a}, which is what I'd expect for a safe-cast rule.\n\nI'm checking the Raise-policy version: the extra field case should error and keep the cast strict, which is what I'd expect. I'm also confirming that same-name casts with non-lossless dtypes like Int64 to Int32 fall back to strict, and that this applies to both the lazy and streaming paths since type coercion happens during IR conversion.\n\nI'm checking the Int64-to-Int8 path: the lossless supertype comes back as Int64, not Int8, so the error branch fires and the cast stays strict. Next I want to see how the struct cast behaves in NonStrict mode when field names don't match, so I'm pulling up th